    <div class="hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">Poecilotheria metallica</div>
      <h1>Bred carefully. Handled gently. <em>Kept for a reason.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">Gootyland is a small <em>Poecilotheria metallica</em> breeding project built around two things: raising genuinely healthy, ethically kept animals, and exploring how their venom might one day help researchers develop real medicine — without the animal paying the cost.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section id="mission">
    <h2>Why this exists</h2>
    <p>Tarantula venom is a real, active area of medical research — researchers have already found compounds in spider venom with genuine potential for pain treatment and other therapies. Most of that research depends on venom collected from wild-caught or poorly-kept animals, under conditions that are hard on the spider and inconsistent for the science.</p>
    <p><strong>Gootyland's long-term goal is to change that input</strong> — animals bred and kept well, with harvesting practices built around minimizing stress rather than maximizing yield, so the venom reaching researchers comes from a genuinely ethical source. That's a long road, and it starts with the basics: healthy breeding, careful husbandry, and getting the practices right before anything scales up.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="husbandry">
    <h2>Husbandry &amp; ethical handling</h2>
    <p><em>P. metallica</em> is a fast, defensive, arboreal species — not a beginner tarantula, and not one that tolerates careless handling well. Good husbandry here means tall, well-ventilated enclosures, stable humidity, and minimizing handling to what's actually necessary rather than what's convenient.</p>

    <figure class="husbandry-figure">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="A young Poecilotheria metallica sling next to an adult, showing the real size difference between a juvenile and its parent">
      <figcaption>A sling next to its mother — the size difference is real, not a trick of the camera. Slings this size need very different housing and handling than an adult.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>Educational video content on husbandry and low-stress handling is planned for this section — real footage, not stock imagery, showing how these animals are actually kept and worked with here.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="animals">
    <h2>Current animals</h2>
    <div class="status-card">
      <div class="tag">Rebuilding</div>
      <p>The collection is coming back from a peak breeding year that produced close to 1,000 slings. Right now that means mostly established adult females, plus juveniles at around 15-18 months, with numbers being built back up deliberately rather than rushed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="shop">
    <h2>Individual listings</h2>
    <div class="status-card">
      <div class="tag">Coming soon</div>
      <p>A simple, personal listing page is planned here — not a full marketplace, just this collection's own available animals as they come up, the way a small breeder's page should work.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
